<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yongcheng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">677–383 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yongcheng, or Yong, was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> from 677 to 383 BCE, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fengxiang">Fengxiang</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>. Excavations have revealed its walls, palace and temple foundations and the great tomb attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-jing-of-qin">Duke Jing of Qin</a>, the largest pre-imperial tomb excavated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> rulers continued to perform major sacrifices there after the capital moved east.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yongcheng.jpg" alt="Yongcheng" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Slb nsk · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ChinaHenanYongcheng.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
